"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][34]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M34-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M34-L1 – Specification of a Mixed-Signal Measurement Chain: Noise and Error Budget<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master’s Specialisation: Design of Electronic Systems and Integrated Circuits<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive an error budget from the\nsensor source to the digital result and distinguish deterministic errors\nfrom noise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the measurement objective to the interface<\/h2>\n<p>A mixed-signal system connects continuous electrical quantities to\ndiscrete codes. The interface is not fully defined until the measurement\nrange, bandwidth, permissible error, update rate and maximum latency are\nknown. For a virtual measurement chain, we choose a bipolar ADC with a\ntotal span of 2 V, 12 bits, 100 kS/s and an input sinusoid with an\namplitude of 0.8 V. These values are modelling assumptions, not a\nproduct selection. An analogue amplifier makes use of the available\nrange; a low-pass filter limits noise and aliasing. The digital side\nprocesses codes and overload status.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_100_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Signal flow from the sensor to the digital word and contributions to input-referred RMS noise.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Sensor, amplifier, analog filter and ADC jointly\ndetermine an input-referred error budget; total RMS noise is combined\nfrom several distinct contributions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Noise budget and reference plane<\/h2>\n<p>An amplifier with gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransfers both the input signal and input-referred noise. Uncorrelated\nerror powers add, rather than their RMS amplitudes. For an\ninput-referred voltage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a downstream contribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ADC input has<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}^2=G^2\\sigma_s^2+\\sigma_a^2+q^2/12,\\qquad q=V_{\\mathrm{FS}}/2^N.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The quantisation approximation assumes sufficiently distributed\nquantisation phases, no overload and uniform step widths. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_s=20\\,\\mu\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_a=90\\,\\mu\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>488.28<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=488.28\\,\\mu\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is approximately 185.3 µV RMS at the ADC, or 46.3 µV referred\nto the sensor input. A sinusoidal signal with a peak amplitude of 0.8 V\nthus yields an SNR of approximately 69.7 dB. For correlated sources,\ncovariances must also be considered.<\/p>\n<p>For a resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe one-sided thermal open-circuit voltage noise spectral density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4k_BT R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin V²/Hz. An RC low-pass filter with cutoff frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas an equivalent noise bandwidth of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi f_c/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot simply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA 10-kΩ resistor at 300 K with a cutoff frequency of 10 kHz yields\napproximately 1.61 µV RMS if only its own noise source is\nconsidered.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Systematic deviations<\/h2>\n<p>A linear error model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(1+\\epsilon_g)x+b+e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith an offset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV and a gain error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon_g=0.001<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe maximum static error at an input magnitude of 0.8 V is 1.4 mV before\nfurther contributions. Errors of known signs may partly cancel; a\nworst-case budget must not assume such cancellation. Unknown systematic\nerrors acting in the same direction are not treated as independent\nrandom quantities.<\/p>\n<p>A two-point calibration estimates offset and slope using known\nreference voltages. It does not correct a curved transfer\ncharacteristic, a timing offset or saturation. Evaluating a calibration\non the same data used to determine it is not an independent test.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Specification matrix<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion<\/th>\n<th>Model target<\/th>\n<th>Test method<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Total span<\/td>\n<td>−1 to +1 V<\/td>\n<td>Ramp test, limits<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sampling<\/td>\n<td>100 kS/s<\/td>\n<td>Timing budget<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dynamic performance at approx. 1 kHz<\/td>\n<td>SINAD at least 65 dB<\/td>\n<td>Coherent sine-wave test<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uncalibrated total deviation<\/td>\n<td>RMS no greater than 1 mV<\/td>\n<td>Comparison with ideal input<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overload<\/td>\n<td>None under nominal operation<\/td>\n<td>Input and code limits<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conversion plus acquisition<\/td>\n<td>No more than 10 µs<\/td>\n<td>Timing model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The total RMS error target includes offset, gain and phase, some of\nwhich are removed by the sine-wave fit. The SINAD target alone is\ntherefore not an absolute accuracy specification. The limits are\nexplicitly tested in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M34-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Double G to 8 with the noise sources unchanged.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Referred to the input, the contribution of\nthe downstream sources decreases; at the ADC, the noise rises to\napproximately 231 µV. However, the sensor range must be halved if the\nADC span remains unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add two offsets of 0.4 mV and 0.6 mV acting entirely in the same\ndirection. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 1 mV, not 0.72 mV.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a high sampling rate not replace an analogue anti-aliasing\nfilter? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Frequencies outside the usable\nNyquist band can fold into the measurement band; subsequent digital\nseparation is generally not possible.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=122815&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=en&amp;seite=0&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Electronics and Photonics (Curriculum G4)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Autumn 2026,\nElectronics and Photonics track: 227-0146-00L Data Conversion System\nDesign; curricular comparison only, no proof of individual equations or\nexperiment. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/technical-articles/defining-and-testing-dynamic-parameters-in-highspeed-adcs-part-1.html\">Analog\nDevices – Defining and Testing Dynamic Parameters in High-Speed ADCs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· SINAD and ENOB sections: SINAD conversion correct; first RMS-error\nequation conflicts with accompanying text. ENOB conversion cross-checked\nseparately (NI). · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M34-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M34-L2 – Data Converters: Sampling, Quantisation, Linearity Errors and Effective Resolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master’s Specialisation: Design of Electronic Systems and Integrated Circuits<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M34-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate code transitions and\ndynamic ADC metrics, distinguish test methods and derive jitter\nlimits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sampling and code assignment<\/h2>\n<p>The sample-and-hold circuit stores an analogue voltage for\nconversion. An ideal bipolar N-bit converter with total span\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{FS}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncodes and step width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=V_{\\mathrm{FS}}/2^N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor midpoint reconstruction and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-V_{\\mathrm{FS}}/2\\le x&lt;V_{\\mathrm{FS}}/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{x+V_{\\mathrm{FS}}/2}{q}\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad \\hat x=-V_{\\mathrm{FS}}/2+(c+1/2)q.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Outside this range, we clip the result to the end codes; the error\nbound of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not apply there. At 12 bits and 2 V,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.48828125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0.48828125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, the result is code 2252 and a reconstructed value of 99.8535 mV.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_101_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ideal and shifted transitions, DNL and INL of a monotonic transfer characteristic model.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Shifted quantization transitions alter code widths;\nDNL describes local step errors and INL the accumulated deviation from\nthe chosen reference characteristic.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Static transfer characteristic deviations<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote the transitions from code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the interior code widths\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_k=T_{k+1}-T_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{DNL}_k=w_k/q-1,\\qquad \\mathrm{INL}_k=(T_k-T_{k,\\mathrm{ref}})/q.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The reference line and treatment of offset and gain must be stated,\nbecause endpoint and best-fit definitions yield different values. An\ninterior code with zero width is missing (DNL = −1). A model with\nstrictly increasing transitions is monotonic; real non-monotonic\nconverters may require a more general description. For a DAC, the\nanalogue output levels and their differences are evaluated instead.<\/p>\n<p>Example: Transition deviations of +0.1, +0.4 and −0.2 LSB relative to\nthe ideal line give DNL values of +0.3 and −0.6 LSB for the two\nintervening codes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamic metrics<\/h2>\n<p>For a statistically uniform quantisation error in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-q/2,q/2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>q<\/mi><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_q^2=\\frac1q\\int_{-q/2}^{q/2}e^2\\,de=q^2/12.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A full-scale sinusoid has an RMS value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{FS}}/(2\\sqrt2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis yields the idealised relationship\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.02<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.76<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SNR}=6.02N+1.76<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB. For a smaller amplitude A, add\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>A<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\log_{10}(2A/V_{\\mathrm{FS}})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a peak amplitude of 0.8 V and 12 bits, approximately 72.1 dB is\nexpected. SINAD also includes harmonic distortion and excludes DC. The\nusual conversion<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.76<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{ENOB}=(\\mathrm{SINAD}-1.76)/6.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>refers to full scale; for a smaller test amplitude, this result must\nexplicitly be identified as an amplitude-uncorrected value. THD\ndescribes only the harmonic components, while SFDR is the separation\nfrom the strongest spurious tone. A sine-wave fit or a coherent FFT\nwithout leakage enables reproducible tests.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sampling jitter and settling<\/h2>\n<p>For a sinusoidal input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=A\\sin(2\\pi ft)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a small independent timing error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nTaylor expansion gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e\\approx x&#39;(t)\\delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe jitter-limited SNR is therefore approximately<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SNR}_j=-20\\log_{10}(2\\pi f\\sigma_t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At 40 kHz and 50 ns RMS, this is approximately 38 dB. A higher\nnominal resolution cannot eliminate this error. For an RC acquisition\ncircuit, however, a constant voltage step\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresults in a residual deviation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V\\exp(-t_a/RC)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two mechanisms require different countermeasures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate q for 16 bits and 2 V. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 30.52\nµV.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A 12-bit ADC achieves a SINAD of 62 dB at full scale.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> ENOB is approximately 10.01 bits.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What jitter limit gives 70 dB at 40 kHz? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\nApproximately 1.26 ns RMS if jitter is the only contributing error.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do 12 output bits not guarantee 12-bit accuracy?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Offset, gain, INL, noise, jitter and\ndistortion remain separate sources of error.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=122815&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=en&amp;seite=0&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Electronics and Photonics (Curriculum G4)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Autumn 2026,\nElectronics and Photonics track: 227-0146-00L Data Conversion System\nDesign; curricular comparison only, no proof of individual equations or\nexperiment. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/technical-articles/defining-and-testing-dynamic-parameters-in-highspeed-adcs-part-1.html\">Analog\nDevices – Defining and Testing Dynamic Parameters in High-Speed ADCs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· SINAD and ENOB sections: SINAD conversion correct; first RMS-error\nequation conflicts with accompanying text. ENOB conversion cross-checked\nseparately (NI). · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z000000P6wbSAC&amp;l=en-US\">NI\n– cDAQ Effective Number of Bits (ENOB)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Solution section: SINAD\nconversion and dependence on signal amplitude and frequency; limited\nformula evidence. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25 · Content verified:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M34-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M34-L3 – ADC and DAC Architectures: Comparing SAR, Pipeline and Delta-Sigma Approaches<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master’s Specialisation: Design of Electronic Systems and Integrated Circuits<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M34-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M31-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare conversion architectures\nin terms of throughput, latency, bandwidth and settling time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>SAR as a binary search<\/h2>\n<p>A SAR ADC (successive approximation register) stores the input, tests\nthe most significant bit first and refines the decision down to the\nleast significant bit. A DAC, comparator and register form a feedback\npath. With ideal transitions, the decision corresponds to a binary\nsearch for the largest threshold that does not exceed the input. A 4-bit\nADC with a range of 0–1 V and an input of 0.70 V tests codes 8, 12, 10\nand 11 in sequence. The thresholds are 0.5, 0.75, 0.625 and 0.6875 V;\nthe result is 11. Each step requires DAC settling time and a reliable\ncomparator decision.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_102_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Binary SAR decision, pipeline with residue and delta-sigma loop with decimation.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: SAR, pipeline and delta-sigma converters process\ninput differently: binary search, staged residue conversion, and\nfeedback quantization followed by decimation, respectively.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pipeline and flash<\/h2>\n<p>A flash ADC compares the input against several thresholds in\nparallel; a simple N-bit flash architecture requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^N-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomparators. In a pipeline, a stage determines coarse partial\ninformation, converts it back to analogue form, subtracts it from the\ninput and amplifies the residue. An idealised stage model is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_{j+1}=G_j(r_j-D_j(c_j)).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Digital correction and calibrated stages can reduce errors but\nrequire redundant ranges or additional information. Once the pipeline is\nfilled, it can deliver a new value every clock period, even though each\nindividual value passes through several stages. Four stages at 10 ns per\nstage give a latency of 40 ns and throughput of 100 MS/s in the\nsimplified model. Register and alignment delays are not included\nhere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Delta-sigma and DAC<\/h2>\n<p>A delta-sigma converter encodes the signal using a high internal\nrate, a loop filter and a feedback DAC. In the linearised model of a\nsimple modulator, the quantisation error is high-pass shaped by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-z^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA digital low-pass filter with decimation reduces high-frequency errors\nand the data rate. For white quantisation noise and an ideal filter,\nwith a first-order modulator and a large oversampling ratio (OSR),\napproximately<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{q,\\mathrm{band}}^2\\approx(q^2/12)\\frac{\\pi^2}{3\\,\\mathrm{OSR}^3}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This relationship is not a guarantee of stability or performance for\nthe real nonlinear modulator; idle tones, overload, non-ideal filters\nand thermal noise can dominate. Increasing the OSR from 16 to 32 reduces\nthe noise power in the model by a factor of 8, or approximately 9\ndB.<\/p>\n<p>A DAC may use an R-2R ladder, capacitive charge redistribution or\ncurrent sources. The digital number determines an analogue output value;\nglitches at the transition 0111→1000 and finite settling time are\ndynamic errors not captured by a purely static transfer characteristic.\nThe feedback DAC of an ADC is subject to the same fundamental\nlimitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Architecture selection<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach<\/th>\n<th>Advantage<\/th>\n<th>Limitation to test<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SAR<\/td>\n<td>Direct individual conversion, efficient binary search<\/td>\n<td>Acquisition and DAC settling time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pipeline<\/td>\n<td>High continuous throughput<\/td>\n<td>Latency, stage errors, power consumption<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delta-sigma<\/td>\n<td>High in-band resolution over a limited bandwidth<\/td>\n<td>Filter latency, settling after a channel change<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flash<\/td>\n<td>Parallel decision<\/td>\n<td>Exponential comparator count<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For the measurement chain from L1, we choose SAR: nominal rate 100\nkS/s, 12 decisions plus two modelled clock cycles of overhead, and 2 µs\nacquisition time. At a SAR clock frequency of 2 MHz, the total time is 9\nµs; at 1 MHz, it is 16 µs. A sufficiently fast conversion model does not\nin itself guarantee that the input circuit has settled.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many comparators does an ideal 8-bit flash ADC require?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 255.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What minimum SAR clock frequency follows from the timing budget?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14/(10-2)\\,\\mu\\mathrm s^{-1}=1.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nMHz.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A decimation filter requires five output values to reach the\nspecified settling accuracy. At 1 kS/s, five periods correspond to 5 ms.\n<strong>Check:<\/strong> Do not confuse this with the internal modulator\nperiod; the exact latency depends on the filter and the definition of\nthe starting point.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=122815&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=en&amp;seite=0&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Electronics and Photonics (Curriculum G4)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Autumn 2026,\nElectronics and Photonics track: 227-0146-00L Data Conversion System\nDesign; curricular comparison only, no proof of individual equations or\nexperiment. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/analog-dialogue/articles/analog-to-digital-converter-architectures-and-choices.html\">Analog\nDevices – Analog-to-Digital Converter Architectures and Choices for\nSystem Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Introduction and architecture comparison: ADC\nselection criteria. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/technical-articles/2022/07/16/07/42/successive-approximation-registers-sar-and-flash-adcs.html\">Analog\nDevices – Understanding SAR ADCs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Abstract and introduction: SAR\nconversion by binary search and comparison of converter architectures. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M34-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M34-L4 – Transistor-Level CMOS Logic: Robustness, Switching Energy and Delay<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master’s Specialisation: Design of Electronic Systems and Integrated Circuits<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Quantitatively assess logic\nlevels, dynamic energy and RC delays for a CMOS stage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Complementary transistors<\/h2>\n<p>The CMOS inverter connects a p-channel MOSFET between the supply and\noutput and an n-channel MOSFET between the output and ground. Both gates\nreceive the same input signal. In a stable binary state, ideally only\none of the pull-up and pull-down paths conducts. During the transition,\nboth may conduct; leakage current and short-circuit current remain real\nloss mechanisms even without a capacitive load.<\/p>\n<p>For NAND, the NMOS transistors in the pull-down path are in series\nand the PMOS transistors in the pull-up path are in parallel. For NOR,\nthe arrangement is reversed. The conducting paths must be checked for\nevery valid input vector. Additional series resistances alter the delay,\nso identical logic behaviour does not imply identical electrical\nperformance.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_103_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"NMOS/PMOS inverter with load capacitance, idealised static transfer characteristic and RC switching response.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The complementary CMOS stage charges and discharges\na capacitive load; static transfer characteristic and RC switching\nresponse illustrate different aspects of the same model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logic levels and noise margins<\/h2>\n<p>The static transfer characteristic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_o(V_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines the boundaries of the valid input ranges. For an inverter,\nthe points at which\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dV_o/dV_i=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare often chosen as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe guaranteed output limits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OL}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be specified under defined load and process conditions.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>N<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NM_L=V_{IL}-V_{OL},\\qquad NM_H=V_{OH}-V_{IH}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IL}=0.40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{IH}=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OL}=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{OH}=1.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV, both noise margins are 0.35 V. These are assumed guaranteed\nabstraction limits, not measurements taken from the figure. A positive\nstatic noise margin prevents neither timing errors nor every form of\nelectromagnetic interference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy per switching cycle<\/h2>\n<p>When a capacitance C is charged from 0 to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{DD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal voltage source supplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">CV_{DD}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHalf of this energy is stored and the other half is dissipated in the\nresistive charging path. During discharge, the stored half is\ndissipated. For a complete 0→1→0 sequence,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=CV_{DD}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDefining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the average number of 0→1 transitions per clock period gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{dyn}=\\alpha C V_{DD}^2 f.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For C = 20 fF, 1.2 V, 100 MHz and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe power at one node is 0.576 µW. Reducing the supply voltage reduces\nthe quadratic term but may increase switching delay and susceptibility\nto interference. This expression does not account for leakage or\nshort-circuit power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Delay and parasitic loading<\/h2>\n<p>An equivalent resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{on}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the total load capacitance provide a first estimate of the output\nstep response. The 50% crossing time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>50<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{50}=\\ln(2)R_{on}C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor 4 kΩ and 20 fF, this is 55.5 ps; doubling the load gives 111 ps. The\n10–90% rise time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(9)RC<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe time required to reach guaranteed valid logic levels may differ from\nthe 50% crossing time.<\/p>\n<p>The approximation assumes a constant switching resistance and lumped\ncapacitance. In practice, conductivity and capacitance vary with\nvoltage; interconnect resistance, coupling, fan-out and process\nvariation influence the transitions. An actual chip design requires\ntransistor models, standard cells and extracted parasitics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reduce the supply voltage from 1.2 to 1.0 V while keeping C, f and\nthe activity factor unchanged. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Dynamic power\nfalls to 69.4%; the delay must not be assumed to remain unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the 10–90% rise time for 4 kΩ and 20 fF.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Approximately 176 ps.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the effect of doubling the load capacitance in the RC model?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Delay and dynamic energy both double.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the static CMOS characteristic insufficient for deciding on a\nclock frequency? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> It contains no propagation\ndelay, capacitance charging, setup or hold requirements.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=122815&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=en&amp;seite=0&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Electronics and Photonics (Curriculum G4)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Autumn 2026,\nElectronics and Photonics track: 227-0147-10L VLSI 3; curricular\ncomparison only, no proof of individual equations or experiment. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-012-microelectronic-devices-and-circuits-fall-2005/resources/lec14/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.012, CMOS inverter, Lecture 14<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture 14 (resource\ndescription): CMOS inverter, noise margins, delay and dynamic power. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M34-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M34-L5 – Integrated Digital Design: Data Paths, Critical Paths and Layout Parasitics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master’s Specialisation: Design of Electronic Systems and Integrated Circuits<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M34-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Assess a synchronous data path,\ndistinguish setup and hold constraints and account for layout effects in\na timing budget.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Registers and data paths<\/h2>\n<p>A synchronous digital design consists of stored states and\ncombinational functions evaluated within a clock cycle. For the ADC\ninterface, registers carry the code word and its validity flag. Between\nthem, operations may include sign extension, correction, limiting and\ncomparison against an alarm threshold. Correct functionality alone is\ninsufficient: the validity signal must correspond to the correct data\nword, including after reset and during gaps in the data stream.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_104_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Register–logic–register data path and maximum and minimum timing constraints.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Between two synchronous registers, maximum logic\ndelay and minimum path delay constrain setup and hold requirements; both\nmust be checked separately.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Setup and the critical path<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{CQ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote the delay from the launching clock edge to the data output,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{pd}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe maximum combinational delay and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe setup time of the receiving register. With no clock skew and an\nexplicitly separate safety margin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following applies:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{clk}\\ge t_{CQ,max}+t_{pd,max}+t_s+m.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For 0.8 ns, 5.3 ns, 0.6 ns and 0.5 ns, the minimum period is 7.2 ns,\ncorresponding to a maximum of 138.9 MHz. At 100 MHz, the setup slack is\n2.8 ns. The critical path is the path with the smallest slack, not\nnecessarily the one with the most logic gates.<\/p>\n<p>The hold condition, by contrast, uses the minimum delay:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{CQ,min}+t_{cd,min}\\ge t_h+m_h.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For 0.15 ns + 0.10 ns and a requirement of 0.35 ns (including\nmargin), there is a hold violation of 0.10 ns. Slowing the clock does\nnot eliminate it in this model. Positive skew at the receiving register\n(its clock edge occurs later than the launching edge) helps setup timing\nbut makes hold timing more demanding. In real timing analysis, skew,\nuncertainty and timing corners are treated systematically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pipelining and interface latency<\/h2>\n<p>Introducing a pipeline boundary in the combinational path can reduce\nthe maximum processing time per stage. If the 5.3 ns is split into 2.8\nand 2.5 ns, unchanged register delays and margin give a limiting clock\nperiod of 4.7 ns, or approximately 212.8 MHz. The value is now available\nonly after two register transfers rather than one. At 200 MHz, the two\ntransfers span two clock periods in total, or 10 ns from the launching\nedge to the final receiving edge. Parallel paths and valid data must be\ndelayed accordingly.<\/p>\n<p>An ADC interface often uses a different clock from the processing\nsystem. A single asynchronous control signal can be synchronised, but\nsynchronising the individual bits of a multi-bit word independently can\nproduce an inconsistent word. Controlled handshakes or asynchronous\nFIFOs address the problem at the interface level; the probability of\nmetastability is not exactly zero.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From RTL to layout<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Design phase<\/th>\n<th>Objective<\/th>\n<th>Example check<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RTL<\/td>\n<td>Functionality and clock structure<\/td>\n<td>Testbench, reference model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Synthesis<\/td>\n<td>Logic network and target technology<\/td>\n<td>Equivalence, area budget<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Placement and routing<\/td>\n<td>Physical connections<\/td>\n<td>Utilisation, DRC<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extraction and timing<\/td>\n<td>Parasitic R/C, PVT corners<\/td>\n<td>Setup, hold, transition times<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sign-off<\/td>\n<td>Consistency of all acceptance criteria<\/td>\n<td>Technology-dependent checks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>PVT stands for process, supply voltage and temperature. A simple\ninterconnect RC model with 1 kΩ and 100 fF gives 69.3 ps to reach 50%. A\nuniformly distributed interconnect has different time constants;\ncoupling also depends on the activity of neighbouring interconnects. The\ncalculated slack must therefore not be treated as sign-off.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An additional maximum path delay of 1.4 ns arises in the original\n100 MHz design. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The setup slack falls from\n2.8 to 1.4 ns.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add at least 0.10 ns of minimum delay to the short hold path.\n<strong>Check:<\/strong> The hold constraint is met numerically; setup\nmust be checked again.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compare the pipeline and single-stage designs at the same sampling\nrate. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Pipelining provides potentially higher\nthroughput but adds registers and latency; at 100 kS/s, the slower data\npath may also suffice.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name three missing prerequisites for actual timing sign-off.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Technology-specific cells/netlist, extracted\nparasitics, complete clock/PVT and timing constraints.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=122815&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=en&amp;seite=0&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Electronics and Photonics (Curriculum G4)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Autumn 2026,\nElectronics and Photonics track: 227-0147-10L VLSI 3; curricular\ncomparison only, no proof of individual equations or experiment. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c5/c5s1/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.004, Sequential Logic<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 5.1 annotated slides: register\nsetup/hold time and clock-period inequalities, relevant to digital\ntiming only. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c7/c7s1/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.004, Performance Measures and Pipelining<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 7.1 annotated\nslides: balanced pipeline stages, critical propagation path, throughput\nand latency; not layout parasitics. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M34-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M34-L6 – Specialisation Project: A Virtual Data Converter Interface from Specification to Verification<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M34 – Master’s Specialisation: Design of Electronic Systems and Integrated Circuits<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M34-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M34&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M34-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Implement a reproducible\nsynthetic SAR measurement chain, isolate error mechanisms and assess\nacceptance limits using independent checks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Project brief and acceptance criteria<\/h2>\n<p>The measurement chain from L1 receives a virtual analogue input\nsignal and maps it to 12 bits. The goal is not a particular ADC chip but\na verifiable specification with separate error metrics. Nominal\nconditions: a 2 V full-scale span, 100 kS/s and a sinusoid of\napproximately 1 kHz with a peak amplitude of 0.8 V. For the following\neducational tests, we require at least 65 dB SINAD, at most 1 mV\nuncalibrated total RMS error, zero overrange samples and a time budget\nof 10 µs. The high-frequency test is a deliberate stress test outside\nthe nominal frequency range.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_105_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"SINAD, total error, clipping and conversion time for six model cases.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Six virtual ADC configurations are assessed for\nSINAD, total error, clipping and conversion time; a favourable\nindividual result does not replace checking every\ncriterion.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model and independent reference<\/h2>\n<p>We generate 8,192 values at 100 kHz; the frequency is coherent with\nthe observation interval:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=k f_s/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith k=83 (1013.18 Hz) or 3277 (40,002.44 Hz). The model includes\nindependent Gaussian amplitude and timing errors. For the acquisition\ncapacitor, we use an input target value held constant during the\nacquisition interval. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=\\exp(-t_a/\\tau)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_n=r v_{n-1}+(1-r)x_n.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is discrete RC acquisition with memory, not a replacement for a\ncontinuous-time SPICE sensor/driver model. A linear offset and gain\nerror are added; the thresholds are shifted by a sinusoidal INL of at\nmost 0.35 LSB relative to the ideal transfer characteristic. The SAR\nalgorithm tests twelve bits in succession. <code>searchsorted<\/code>\nserves as an independent code-assignment reference for the same strictly\nmonotonic transition thresholds. A timing-budget violation is recorded,\nrather than simulating an artificial real-time conversion.<\/p>\n<p>The sine-fit metric separates DC and the fundamental from the\nresidual. Comparison with a coherent FFT verifies the SINAD calculation;\ndirect comparison with the ideal input also captures linear errors. Here\nthe ENOB value deliberately uses the full-scale formula without\namplitude correction. The value is therefore meaningfully comparable\nonly at the stated test level.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible experiment<\/h2>\n<p>Python 3 and NumPy are sufficient. The same seed provides comparable\ndisturbances; noise and distortion are evaluated from a finite set of\nsamples. The experiment stores all codes in RAM and models neither\nclock-domain crossing (CDC) nor actual output protocols. The original\ncode retains the case labels <code>RC langsam<\/code> (slow RC),\n<code>Jitter HF<\/code> (HF jitter), <code>Uebersteuerung<\/code>\n(clipping) and <code>Takt langsam<\/code> (slow clock).<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>FS<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">100_000.<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> N<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8192<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> B<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> VFS<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> Q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>VFS<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>B<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> TACQ<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2e-6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># name, amplitude V, coherent bin, noise rms V, jitter rms s, RC s, offset V, gain error, INL amplitude LSB, SAR clock Hz<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cases<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Ideal&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">83<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e6<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Nominal&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">83<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">120e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e-9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.15e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.0006<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.001<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e6<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;RC langsam&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">83<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">120e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e-9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.0006<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.001<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e6<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Jitter HF&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3277<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">120e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">50e-9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.15e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.0006<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.001<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e6<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Uebersteuerung&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.05<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">83<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">120e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e-9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.15e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.0006<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.001<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e6<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Takt langsam&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">83<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">120e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2e-9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.15e-6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.0006<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.001<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.35<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1e6<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> sar(x,thresholds):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    code<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.zeros(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(x),dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(B<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        trial<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>code<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">&lt;&lt;<\/span>b)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        code<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.where(x<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span>thresholds[trial<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],trial,code)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> code<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> simulate(case):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    name,a,k,noise,jitter,tau,offset,gain,inl,clk<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>case<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">2801<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(N)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>FS<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> phase<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>FS<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>t<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(phase)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(phase<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>FS<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>rng.normal(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,jitter,N))<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>rng.normal(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,noise,N)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> tau:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>TACQ<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>tau)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> prev<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> j <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(N):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            prev<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>r)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v[j]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>prev<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> v[j]<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>prev<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>v<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>gain)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>offset<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    edges<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span>VFS<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>B)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> edges<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span>inl<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>edges<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>VFS<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(np.diff(edges)<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    code<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>sar(v,edges)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.array_equal(code,np.searchsorted(edges,v,side<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;right&#39;<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    y<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span>VFS<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(code<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> clipped<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.count_nonzero((v<span class=\"op\">&lt;-<\/span>VFS<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">|<\/span>(v<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span>VFS<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    M<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.column_stack([np.ones(N),np.sin(phase),np.cos(phase)])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    fit<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.lstsq(M,y,rcond<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">None<\/span>)[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> resid<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>M<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>fit<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    signal<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.hypot(fit[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],fit[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> err<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(np.mean(resid<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> sinad<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">20<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log10(signal<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>err)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(np.mean((y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>fit[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),signal<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>err<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,rtol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"co\"># FFT reference: coherent tone, DC excluded, Parseval; fundamental plus remaining bins<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    spec<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(np.fft.rfft(y))<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> spec[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    fft_sinad<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log10(spec[k]<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(spec[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:].<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>spec[k]))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(fft_sinad<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>sinad)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rms<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(np.mean((y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> timing<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>TACQ<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(B<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>clk<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">dict<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x,y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>y,resid<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>resid,sinad<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>sinad,enob<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(sinad<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.76<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">6.02<\/span>,rms<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rms,gain<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.hypot(fit[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],fit[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>a,phase<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.degrees(np.arctan2(fit[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],fit[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])),clip<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>clipped,timing<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>timing,offset<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>fit[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],code<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>code)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span><span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> cases:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>simulate(c)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&quot;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>c[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"sc\">:16s}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>r[<span class=\"st\">&#39;sinad&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"sc\">:6.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> dB <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>r[<span class=\"st\">&#39;enob&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"sc\">:5.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> bit <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>r[<span class=\"st\">&#39;rms&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e3<\/span><span class=\"sc\">:7.3f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> mV <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(r[<span class=\"st\">&#39;gain&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"sc\">:7.3f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> % <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>r[<span class=\"st\">&#39;phase&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"sc\">:7.3f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> deg <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>r[<span class=\"st\">&#39;clip&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"sc\">:4d}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>r[<span class=\"st\">&#39;timing&#39;<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e6<\/span><span class=\"sc\">:5.1f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> us&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results and interpretation<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-8\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-11\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">SINAD (dB)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">ENOB* (bits)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Total RMS (mV)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Gain error (%)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Phase (°)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Clip<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Time (µs)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ideal<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">71.96<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11.66<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.143<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">68.13<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11.03<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.829<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.094<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.001<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow RC<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">67.78<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10.97<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.681<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.057<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.571<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HF jitter<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">38.12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.04<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.068<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.092<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.003<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clipping<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">34.57<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.45<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16.317<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-1.190<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1632<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow clock<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">68.13<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11.03<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.829<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.094<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">-0.001<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">16.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>*ENOB without amplitude correction; for the clipping test, too, it is\nmerely a calculated value, not a valid nominal specification.<\/p>\n<p>Ideal and Nominal meet all model targets. Slower acquisition remains\nabove 65 dB SINAD but causes a much greater total error due to the\nlinear phase shift and the initial transient. The HF jitter case agrees\nin order of magnitude with the theoretical 38 dB limit. Clipping\ndistorts the amplitude and generates harmonics. The slow SAR clock\nproduces the same virtual code sequence as Nominal but cannot meet the\ntiming requirement: a 16 µs cycle cannot deliver 100 kS/s in this serial\nmodel. This could differ only under an alternative architecture model\nwith parallel converters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Run the code and compare the six cases. <strong>Check:<\/strong> All\nSAR codes agree with the sorted-threshold reference; the FFT and\nsine-fit methods yield SINAD values agreeing to better than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-6}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Remove offset and gain error in the Nominal case.\n<strong>Check:<\/strong> The absolute RMS difference should decrease;\nSINAD is considerably less sensitive to pure linear scaling than this\nabsolute error metric (quantisation and INL may change in the\nprocess).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the maximum possible serial throughput at the slow clock\nrate. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 62.5 kS/s, with no additional waiting\ntimes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add a sweep over the acquisition time and derive a compromise\nbetween residual error and conversion time. <strong>Assessment:<\/strong>\nA reproducible parameter set, separate checks of both criteria, and no\npresentation of stress tests as nominal measurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Create a digital interface specification with <code>data<\/code>,\n<code>valid<\/code>, reset and an explicit error indication; define two\ntests each for clipping and missed deadlines. <strong>Check:<\/strong> No\nsilent processing of old data as a new valid result; rules for reset and\ndata loss are unambiguous.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Actual verification requires reference voltages, a suitable test\nsetup, real clock and temperature conditions, hardware/RTL testing,\ndriver models and extracted circuit parameters. This assignment verifies\nonly the documented virtual model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=122815&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=en&amp;seite=0&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Electronics and Photonics (Curriculum G4)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Autumn 2026,\nElectronics and Photonics track: 227-0146-00L Data Conversion System\nDesign; 227-0116-00L VLSI 1; curricular comparison only, no proof of\nindividual equations or experiment. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/technical-articles/defining-and-testing-dynamic-parameters-in-highspeed-adcs-part-1.html\">Analog\nDevices – Defining and Testing Dynamic Parameters in High-Speed ADCs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· SINAD and ENOB sections: SINAD conversion correct; first RMS-error\nequation conflicts with accompanying text. ENOB conversion cross-checked\nseparately (NI). · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.analog.com/en/resources/technical-articles/2022/07/16/07/42/successive-approximation-registers-sar-and-flash-adcs.html\">Analog\nDevices – Understanding SAR ADCs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Abstract and introduction: SAR\nconversion by binary search and comparison of converter architectures. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-004-computation-structures-spring-2017/pages/c5/c5s1/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.004, Sequential Logic<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 5.1 annotated slides: register\nsetup/hold time and clock-period inequalities, relevant to digital\ntiming only. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z000000P6wbSAC&amp;l=en-US\">NI\n– cDAQ Effective Number of Bits (ENOB)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Solution section: SINAD\nconversion and dependence on signal amplitude and frequency; limited\nformula evidence. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25 · Content verified:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
